=== 21:22 UTC 2026-09-20 PRE-REGISTRATION -- THE VETO DOOR: what gpt-5.6-luna does after a refused stop, under each candidate build (capture and fork; arms are builds)
Question: after a Stop hook refuses a stop and the harness delivers the skill the hook named, which change to Zak
Code -- if any -- gets gpt-5.6-luna (served posture, reasoning effort none) back to the loop's own work instead of
answering in words again? This block fixes the instrument, the arms, the outcome, the gates and the reading BEFORE any
scored call. No scored call has been made.

Why this bench exists. The served-luna log named it in advance (rule G of the 2026-09-19 pair): if D1 missed on three
or more veto-door pointers, the next product change was "the body at the veto door", tested by a pre-registered door
bench with the arms "this build / body at the veto door", not by another served sample. D1 then missed: on the control
run (effort none) a veto-door pointer was followed by a work call 4 times of 8, three of four turns ended veto_stall,
turn 1 went 3 deliveries, 3 pointers, 0 skills run to a 600 s rest in 46 s, and 73.2% of the run made no model call.
Both of rule G's arms are here (A and B), read exactly as rule G meant. Six more arms and a placebo are added, each
read against A alone, so their presence changes nothing about how B is read.

What was found while building the instrument, and is NOT evidence yet. One unscored pilot capture (below) stopped AT
the fork, so no arm's outcome has been seen by anyone. Its fork request ends with three messages: the model's closing
words, the harness's delivery of the skill (55,461 characters, closing with the instruction to carry it out and not
summarize), and then the product's own one-line reminder for a FINISHED plan: plan complete, answer the original
request now, lead with the conclusion. That reminder is ephemeral: it rides each request and is written to no session
file and no trace, which is why four served samples never showed it. Two arms (R and P) exist because of it. Whether
it matters is what the bench is for; nothing here says it does.

Instrument. bench/veto_door.py, bench/veto_door_world.py and bench/veto_door_arms/*.patch at the hashes in the
MANIFEST lines under this block. Capture and fork:
  capture  one live run of the real product in the served agent factory's posture (zakpick: deep_code, plan,
           quick_code, summarize and delegate on gpt-5.6-luna with a 163,840 window, classify on gpt-5-nano, fallback
           gpt-5-mini; permission mode autonomous; lean rules; skills, rules and compaction on; streamed turns; the
           workspace's own session store), opened the way a typed "/cycle loop" is, recorded call by call by a tape on
           the provider class, and cancelled at the first tools-bearing call after the FIRST refused stop's delivery.
           That call is the FORK. Only a run's first delivery is a fork, so the prefix owes nothing to any arm.
           Captures are always the unpatched build's.
  rollout  the same world rebuilt at the same path, the capture's session id and model pinned, every recorded
           completion served from the tape (each replayed request must match the recorded one in roles, block kinds,
           tool names and call ids, or the rollout is invalid; every character is compared too and a mismatch makes
           the row unusable), live from the fork on. Every arm therefore starts from the same conversation,
           workspace, plan, fence count and hook budget, rebuilt by the product itself.
  arms are BUILDS  a detached worktree of this commit plus the arm's patches, run as its own process with PYTHONPATH
           on its src. Nothing is switched at run time and nothing is monkeypatched: what is measured could ship.
The world is synthesized and carries no framework text: a stock-recount loop (skill "cycle", argument "loop", a
54,851-character body whose first step is `bash scripts/cycle-open.sh`; a sub-skill "cycle-check"; eight shell
scripts that are the only writers of the files they own; twenty queued lots) and a shell Stop hook that refuses the
stop up to six times, naming the skill re-entry in the words a perpetual-loop framework uses. 18 files, digest
0f58ab7a5c10c328a451e0b145e3309f651a1dfeafc5d81a429a1dc9b4ca2a56. Workspaces are built under /srv/zc-door, outside
every repository, because the product folds project guides from the workspace up to the repository root.
Tapes, request snapshots and completion text stay in a scratch directory and are never committed. Ledger rows carry
labels, counts, hashes, usage and the wire's URL path and four body fields (effort, tool_choice, tool count, model),
never a header and never prompt text.

Arms (the letter is the patch; a two-letter arm carries both):
  A   this build.
  A2  the PLACEBO: this build again, under another name, read against A like any arm. It can never be selected.
  R   the finished plan's "answer now" reminder is silent once a stop has been refused on that plan, and speaks again
      the moment the plan changes. Nothing else about the plan changes.
  P   the finished plan leaves the board when a stop is refused, as it already does at a turn start. This removes the
      reminder too, and ALSO empties the board, so the product's plan-first gate withholds an unplanned command: the
      offline selftest shows a direct first step refused under P and run under every other arm. R and P differ by
      that.
  C   the "[already loaded]" pointer repeats the skill's first step (at most 2,000 characters) instead of naming it.
  F   after a delivery, completions carry tool_choice "required" until the model has run one work call, for at most
      3 completions. The engine binds where the delivery's words are advisory.
  B   the fallback named in advance: the first Skill call for the delivered skill, with nothing run since, is answered
      with the body again, once per delivery. It does not reset the veto fence.
  RC, RF   R with C, R with F.

Outcome of one rollout (one SEGMENT: from the delivery to the first of these):
  pass            the loop RESUMED: a call to one of the loop's own commands (scripts/*.sh) came back from the product
                  without an error. A call the product withheld or refused is an error result, as a failed script is.
  stopped-again   the model ended in words and the hook refused the stop a second time.
  cap             6 live completions and the loop had not resumed (a plan or skill spiral, or reading without end).
  ended:<reason>  the turn ended by itself.
  invalid:<why>   the replay left its tape, or the process did not import its own tree's package.
The product sends luna no temperature and no seed (max_tokens 8192, provider defaults otherwise), so repetitions of
one fork are samples, not copies; they still share a conversation, so the FORK is the unit of every test, and each
fork sits at its own unpinned temporary path.
Reading a file is acting and is NOT resuming; an echo is neither. Both leave the segment open. This is deliberately
wider than rule G's single-step D1 ("the next completion is a work call"): the product's rendering of a long body asks
for a plan FIRST, and arm P makes the model plan before it may act, so a one-completion measure would mis-score
exactly the arms under test. Secondary, reported and deciding nothing: any-work rate, the completion the pass came on,
first act, what followed a door answer, skill-again spirals, echoes, live completions, prompt tokens and cost per
rollout, and the harness's own notes per arm.

A row is USABLE only if all of these hold, and none of them looks at what the model did:
  build     the process imported the package beside its own bench file, and its source diff hash is its arm's.
  prefix    every replayed call matched its recording character for character, all of them were replayed, and the
            fork request is byte-identical to the capture's -- except under R and P where the plan was finished at the
            fork, where it must NOT be (the reminder is what those patches remove).
  wire      every tools-bearing request went to /v1/responses at effort none on gpt-5.6-luna with tool_choice auto
            (or required, under F only), and no live completion came from the fallback model.
  witness   every patch shows itself in its own arm's rows and in no other's: R and P by their trace note and the
            reminder's absence from the fork request, F by its note and "required" on the first live call, C by the
            first step in a veto-door pointer, B by its redelivery note (at most one per delivery).
  outcome   not invalid, not a provider error, not the product's own budget stop.
An unusable cell is run once more and no further. A CELL is (fork, arm, repetition); it is filled by its first usable
row, and a child that died without writing a row leaves its cell unfilled. A fork arm A could not fill is a bad fork,
not a bad arm, so an arm is judged on the forks A filled: with under 90% of its cells filled there it is VOID. A fork
is COMPLETE when A and every arm that is not VOID have all of their cells on it. Every rate and every comparison is
over the complete forks only, so all arms are read on the same conversations.

Gates before the first scored call (a failed gate is fixed and re-run; it spends nothing scored):
  W0  the offline selftest passes in the baseline's built tree (it captures on a scripted model), and then in EVERY
      arm's built tree against that capture: the baseline's tape replays under each arm's build message for message,
      each patch shows its witness, no patch shows another's, the fence still counts, and the reading rule returns its
      known answers on synthetic ledgers.
  W1  preflight, offline: each luna category would send effort none with tools and none without, tool_choice auto,
      "required" when asked, store false, no api_base.
  W2  probe, two live calls (unscored, about $0.0002): tool_choice "required" reaches the wire and comes back as a
      tool call. If it does not, F and RF are dropped BEFORE the batch and recorded as untestable on this route.

Batch 1, the SCREEN. Up to 14 capture runs for 10 forks; 9 arms; 2 repetitions: 180 rollouts. Inside a fork the arms
are interleaved and the starting arm moves one along per fork and per repetition; forks run four at a time.
Denominators, fixed now: 20 rollouts an arm at 10 complete forks (one rollout = 5 points), 14 at the floor of 7 forks
(7.1 points); a fork's own share is 0, 50 or 100%. Read:
  NOT MEASURED        fewer than 7 complete forks. Nothing is concluded about any arm; the instrument is fixed and
                      the batch re-registered. No batch 2.
  NOT DISCRIMINATING  arm A resumes on 85% or more of its rollouts. The door is not where this world loses the loop;
                      no arm is read. No batch 2.
  NOT MEASURED        also if the placebo reads GAIN or HARM: a verdict from nothing means this batch's scale turns
                      noise into verdicts, and no other verdict in it can be believed.
  otherwise each arm X is read against A over the complete forks, d = X's pooled pass rate minus A's, p = an exact
  two-sided sign-flip permutation test over the per-fork differences (the fork is the unit: rollouts of one fork share
  a conversation), in this order:
    VOID    under 90% of its cells filled (on the forks A filled).
    HARM    d <= -15 points AND p < 0.05.
    GAIN    d >= +20 points AND p < 0.05.
    FLAT    |d| < 10 points.
    MIXED   anything else.
  A GAIN or HARM must also stand with any ONE fork left out: d is re-taken on each set of forks-minus-one, and if it
  falls back across its threshold on any of them the arm reads MIXED and the forks it rested on are named. (The p is
  not re-taken on the smaller sets: losing a fork costs any test power and says nothing about one fork carrying it.)
  Then: if any arm GAINs, ONE arm goes to batch 2 -- the first in the order R, P, C, RC, F, RF, B (least product
  change first) among the GAIN arms within 10 points of the best GAIN arm. If none GAINs, the MIXED arms that read
  ABOVE the baseline go forward, the two with the largest d at most (ties: that order). If no arm is GAIN or MIXED
  above the baseline there is no batch 2: nothing ships, rule G's fallback is FALSIFIED on its own test if B read
  FLAT, HARM or MIXED below the baseline (and is said to be UNTESTED if B was VOID), and the next lever is the one
  already named -- reasoning depth for the one completion after a delivery -- under its own pre-registration.
  Seven arms are read at p < 0.05 each, so the screen alone ships nothing: it only picks what batch 2 tests.

Batch 2, the CONFIRMATION (second-sample rule). Fresh captures (up to 12 runs for 8 forks), arms A and A2 plus the one
or two arms batch 1 sent forward, 3 repetitions (24 rollouts an arm at 8 forks, one rollout = 4.2 points; 18 at the
floor), read ALONE and never pooled with batch 1. Same usability rules, same gates; NOT MEASURED below 6 complete
forks or if the placebo reads CONFIRMED, NOT DISCRIMINATING at 85%.
  CONFIRMED       d >= +15 points AND p < 0.05, standing with any one fork left out.   NOT CONFIRMED   anything else.
  One arm CONFIRMED: it ships. Two: the earlier in the order above ships, unless the other leads it by more than 10
  points, then that one. None: nothing ships from this bench and the next lever is the one named above. A NOT
  MEASURED batch 2 may be re-run once on fresh captures; a second one ends the bench with nothing shipped.
"Ships" means built properly on main afterwards: no flag, one behaviour, tests including the deterministic re-spiral
test that the fence still ends veto_stall at three, an ADR, and the next served sample reads whether the rests went.
An outcome not named in this block is never upgraded to "ships".

What the numbers will NOT show, kept apart from the rule on purpose. A GAIN for R or P is what the reminder
hypothesis predicts, and is also what "any change to the last message of the request" would predict; the bench does
not separate the two, and P changes the board as well. The numbers decide what ships, not why it works. The segment
ends at the first resumed command: whether the loop then KEEPS running is the next served sample's question, not this
bench's. One synthesized world, one model, one effort, the first refused stop of each run only: nothing here is a rate
for the framework's own skills, for terra, for the pod tier, or for later stops in a long run.

My estimates, recorded so they can be scored: arm A resumes on 30% of its rollouts (NOT DISCRIMINATING 5%; NOT
MEASURED 12%, of which the placebo 4%). GAIN in batch 1: R 45%, P 35%, C 30%, F 35%, B 35%, RC 50%, RF 50%; at least
one arm GAINs 65%; an arm sent forward is CONFIRMED 60% of the time one is sent.

The pilot, declared. One capture was taken on 2026-09-20 to shake the instrument down (unpatched build 9a0c8fa, world
digest as above): 14 streamed calls, all on /v1/responses at effort none with tool_choice auto, 85% of prompt tokens
read from cache, $0.0192, 33.9 s, labels plan, first-step, skill-other, plan, script, plan, script, script, plan,
script, plan, first-step, plan, text-only; fork at call 14 with a finished 5/5 plan and the reminder as the last
message. It stopped at the fork, no rollout has ever been run against it, and it is EXCLUDED: both batches capture
afresh.

Spend. Hard bounds the orchestrators enforce by not launching past them: batch 1 captures $1.00 and rollouts $2.50,
batch 2 captures $0.75 and rollouts $1.25; each run also carries the product's own cost stop ($0.40 a capture, $0.60
a rollout). Expected about half of that. Campaign live spend to date approximately $6.08, pilot included.

Pre-apply consultation (two queries, subject and mechanism, before this block was fixed). It changed the rule three
ways: a verdict from seven to ten forks is re-taken with each fork left out; the placebo arm exists because a
registered quantity can be noise at the registered scale, and only a control inside the batch shows it; denominators
are written down here, before anyone counts. It also confirmed two choices already made: forks at unpinned paths with
the fork as the unit of analysis, and a wire check rather than a client library's word for what was sent.

