My estimates, scored. "Arm A resumes on 30% of its rollouts": a MISS by 59 points (88.9%). NOT DISCRIMINATING: I gave
it 5%, and it is what happened. NOT MEASURED, 12%: did not happen. The per-arm GAIN estimates and "at least one arm
GAINs, 65%" cannot be scored, because no arm was read; they are not counted as hits. Why the first one missed: I
carried the served control's numbers (a pointer followed by a work call 4 times of 8, three turns of four ending in a
veto stall) into a world I had built myself, and that world is easier at exactly that point (below). A base rate
measured in one world is not a prior for another. A rule whose first branch is a ceiling on the baseline needs the
baseline measured in THIS world first, alone, under its own registration: arm A by itself shows no arm's outcome to
anyone and costs a ninth of a screen.

What the instrument got wrong or could not do, found by reading the unusable rows. None of it touches arm A's cells,
so none of it changes the reading.
  1. One fork in ten could not be replayed even by the build that captured it (all nine arms' 4 rows on it refused
     as "prefix", 36 rows). In that capture the model had asked the product for its plan's history (plan_recall),
     and that result prints each plan event's clock time. A replay re-runs the tool at a later minute: same roles,
     same sizes, different characters, from call 38 of 49 on. Reproduced after the batch by a dry replay that refuses
     its first live call: 49 calls replayed, 11 differing (38 to 48), 0 requests on the wire, and the fork request
     differs from the capture's in exactly one message, that tool result. The usability rule did its job; a capture
     that holds a clock cannot be a fork until the replay freezes it.
  2. Arm C changes a text one capture already held. That run had met a pointer BEFORE its fork, so under C the
     replayed prefix itself differs (18 of 19 calls) and C and RC lose that fork to "prefix" (8 rows each). The
     design assumed a patch acts only after the fork; a patch to a text the prefix can contain does not.
  3. The C witness could not fire at the door, in any arm. The detector asked whether the door's answer OPENS with
     the pointer's tag; the product writes a call's argument ahead of that tag, and this model always passes the
     argument. The flag read false on every door answer of the batch, so every C and RC row whose first act was the
     skill call was refused (36 rows) and the rows kept were the ones where the patch could not act: usability ended
     up depending on what the model did, which the registration says it never may. The patch itself was live: a C or
     RC door answered 1,139 characters carrying the first step, every other pointer 563 without it (B's door answers
     with the body, 55,314). The offline proof missed this because its scripted model calls the skill with no
     argument and its C check looked for the first step only: the positive control was missing for the one form the
     live model uses. With a pointer recognised as "the door's answer that is not the body" and every other rule
     unchanged, the batch reads the same (NOT DISCRIMINATING; A, A2, R, P, F, RF and B cell for cell identical), C
     becomes 12 of 16 and RC 16 of 16, and both stay under 90% filled because of item 2. Printed beside the
     registered reading, as the rule for a corrected instrument asks; it decides nothing.

Found on the way: a product fault, not a bench result. The detector's mistake in item 3 is also the product's. The
Skill tool decides whether what it is about to return is a pointer by testing how the text OPENS, and when the call
carries an argument the product writes the argument's frame ahead of the pointer's tag. So a pointer for a call WITH
an argument is handled as a fresh body. A whole-body skill's pointer gains the follow-these-instructions hint: the
563 characters measured at every unpatched door here are the 456-character pointer plus that 107-character line. A
PAGED skill's pointer, which exists to hand the current section over again, is fed back through the pager: offline,
on a 32k window, a model on section 2 of 4 that asks again with an argument gets that section headed a second time,
as page 1 of 2, without its closing line (the one that says how section 3 arrives), under the first-load hint: its
plan now holds two sections, this is SECTION 1, carry out section 1. Without an argument both cases are correct. A
perpetual-loop framework's re-entry always carries an argument, and the one pointer left in the served control's
session file shows the same stray line. In the product since 2026-08-28 (the pager's change, #275, brought the test;
the argument's frame is older, #210). It goes to main by itself, under its own ADR with deterministic tests: it
is a plain fault, not one of this bench's levers, and nothing here measures what it does to a model.

Secondary, reported and deciding nothing (first tries only, rows whose prefix replayed cleanly: 18 an arm, 16 for C
and RC).
  The door here is easier than the served one. After a veto-door pointer the unpatched build's next completion was a
  work call 22 times of 22 (A 11 of 11, A2 11 of 11). The served control read 4 of 8, with another instrument in
  another world: the two are not one series, but they are far enough apart to say this world does not reproduce the
  served door.
  First act of the unpatched build (A and A2, 36 rollouts): the skill call 22, the loop's first step directly 6,
  words 4, a plan call 3, other work 1.
  The loop's first step usually FAILS here, and the failure hands over the recovery. Of 206 calls that ran it, 132
  came back as errors, 128 of them showing the script's own line, and that script has one refusal: a cycle is
  already open, close it first, naming the command. 203 calls carried a pass in the 200 passing rollouts:
  cycle-close.sh alone 94, cycle-open.sh alone 51, cycle-select.sh alone 17, the first four scripts in one call 15,
  the three checks in one call 11, others 15. A world whose first error names the next command is a world that is
  easy to resume, whatever the door says.
  What followed the door's answer (the next completion is a work call): A 11 of 11, A2 11 of 11, C 15 of 15, F 8 of
  8, RF 14 of 14, RC 19 of 21, R 10 of 14, P 3 of 13, B 0 of 10. Under B the body came back and the next completion
  was a plan call 9 times of 10 and words once: a long body is rendered with a request to plan first, so a second
  body buys a second plan. Under P the emptied board makes the plan-first gate ask for a plan (10 of 13), as the
  selftest showed it would. B cost $0.0079 a rollout against $0.0047 for A.
  Where the second refusals sat. Arms that kept the finished plan's reminder and forced no tool (A, A2, B, C): 16
  rollouts of 70 did not resume, all 16 a second refused stop, 10 of them words as the very first act. Arms where
  the reminder was gone (R, P, RC, RF): 1 of 70, a cap under P, and words first 0 of 70. F: 1 of 18. THIS IS NOT A
  READING. It pools arms after seeing them, it was no registered contrast, and at this ceiling the registered one
  could not be run. It is written down because it is the shape the reminder hypothesis predicted, so that the next
  registration can test it: on the failure this world does produce, a second refused stop, not on a gain the ceiling
  leaves no room for.

What the numbers do NOT show. Nothing about any arm: not that R works, not that B fails. Nothing about the served
loop: the unpatched build passed this world's door 22 times of 22 and the served one 4 of 8, so whatever stalls the
served loop at its door is not reproduced by a 55,000-character synthesized skill, a finished plan and a refused
stop alone. Two differences are visible and unmeasured: here the first step's refusal names the next command, and
the segment allows six completions where the served measure allowed one. One world, one model, one effort, and the
first refused stop of a run only.

Spend. Batch 1: W2 $0.000052, captures $0.2867, rollouts $0.9515, $1.2383 in all against a $3.50 bound. The dry
replay made no provider call. Campaign live spend to date approximately $7.32.

Next. No batch 2 and nothing ships from this bench, as registered. The product fault goes to main by itself. The
instrument is repaired before it is used again: the pointer detector, with a scripted call that carries an argument
as its positive control, and a clock the replay can freeze. Any further measurement of the door is registered
first, in its own block, and starts with the baseline alone.

